what's up guys Greg Knuckles here with  string theory comm and today we asked  ourselves the question which are better  full-body or split routines actually we  don't first we ask ourselves the  question is that even the right question  we should be asking is that a question  that's likely to give us a useful answer  and I would argue no the reason why is  that your response to training isn't  static so there is there is no single  best training routine for everyone in  the world or even just for you at all  points in time because as you train your  body it adapts in in two different ways  one the stressor provokes an adaptation  that is positive it makes you bigger  stronger etc the stuff you want to  happen but also because it's a stress or  your your body becomes more efficient  and handling that same stressor in the  future because remember getting bigger  and stronger is primarily a protective  response because when you train you  disrupt homeostasis and so your body  makes your muscles bigger and stronger  so they'll they'll be more capable of  handling that stressor in the future but  also other protective mechanisms are in  play that makes that particular type of  stressor disrupt homeostasis less than  it used to so when you when you're  training a particular way and you  plateau the well the number one thing  you should do well you can do two  different things you can either increase  the type of stressor or you can increase  the magnitude of the stressor so  increasing the magnitude of the  stressors is primarily just ramping up  your training volume you know adding  weight adding sets adding reps etc but  you can't do that forever obviously and  so at some point you're going to need to  change some of those other variables  and this this leads into the question of  whether you're better off doing  full-body or split routines now in  general you're probably better off  spending most of your time or a larger  percentage of your time doing full-body  routines not necessarily every muscle  group every day but including both some  upper and lower body lifts because  that's that's necessarily well it's not  necessarily but it's going to give you  opportunities to increase how frequently  you're training each muscle in each lift  and in general training something more  frequently to a point is a good thing to  do a few different things are in play  muscle protein synthesis isn't elevated  for too terribly long after a workout 36  to 48 hours for a fairly new lifter  tends to be somewhere closer to 12 to 24  hours for a more experienced lifters so  if you're only training a lift once a  week muscle protein synthesis isn't  elevated for for that large of a  fraction of time you get more  opportunities to practice the lifts  you're training to master them a few  other things but those are the two  Biggie's so in general higher training  frequencies tend to be better and so you  know you could train squats and bench  say both three times a week if you're  training six days a week but if you're  training three four days a week then  generally a full body routine is going  to allow for higher frequency but once  your body adapts to that level of  frequency then you have to ask yourself  a question  you know first can I add more volume per  session and you can do that for a while  but when it gets to the point that you  can't do that then you're probably  better off dialing back your frequency  because even though in general it  doesn't carry those those same  particular advantages the advantage that  it does have is if you're only say  squatting once or twice a week you know  you may be able to do 8 10 12 sets of  squats and those workouts whereas you  probably wouldn't want to do that same  thing four or five days a week so the  per session volume can be  higher and so if you've gotten to the  point where the volume you can handle  for a lift so I'll just use squats as an  example so if you've gotten to the point  where you're training the squat three  four times a week and you've raised the  volume as high as you possibly can for  those three four sessions and your squat  still isn't improving then what you can  do is you can dial back the frequency  and that's going to allow you to push  the per session volume higher and so  obviously since since you weren't  adapting since you weren't getting  stronger then whatever you were doing in  those three four days a week apparently  wasn't enough to sufficiently disrupt  homeostasis to cause a beneficial  adaptive response  so with dialing back the frequency you  can increase the volume per session and  then even though you're not getting as  many sessions disrupting homeostasis  causing those beneficial effects the  sessions that you are getting in do have  high enough volume to sufficiently  disrupt homeostasis help you get a  bigger and stronger then once you've  done that for a while your muscles are  you know you're going to plateau  eventually and your muscles have what  they've adapted to as a higher per  session volume but a lower frequency so  then you can remain frequency back up  keep getting bigger and stronger so like  I said at the start of the video the  question itself isn't a particularly  good question if the question you asked  was what what on average has more  benefits higher or lower frequency  training or full or full body or split  training you know there's an answer to  that question it tends to be full body  higher frequency per lift training but  even though even though it has some  advantages that doesn't necessarily mean  it's better for everyone one because  it's not going to be some people's  preferences - it's not going to fit in  some people's schedules but then even if  you know it does fit your preference it  does fit in your schedule that still  doesn't mean that it is necessarily  better at all points in time it may be  better for you the majority of the time  but once once you've been doing it for a  while once you've adapted to it once you  hit a plateau then almost by  definition it's no longer the best thing  for you because you've just reached a  point that continuing to train in that  same manner no longer gives you the  ability to disrupt homeostasis enough to  tell your muscles to keep getting bigger  and stronger so so that's the basic idea  a paper that I would really recommend  everyone watching this video read it's  called periodization paradigms in the  21st century evidence led our tradition  driven it's by strength coach and  periodization theorist John Kiley  I very strongly recommended one of the  things that that he very rightly points  out is that most studies comparing  different periodization schemes or  training programs the the biggest  takeaway from most of them is just when  you put people on a training program  that is significantly different from  what they were doing before they tend to  get bigger and stronger so you know with  with a lot of the training programs that  are higher frequency programs in the  studies that actually went through and  described the subjects training  backgrounds most of them had lower  frequency training backgrounds so the  way the studies were set up it was  basically with one group training  essentially how they'd been training  before very similarly and another group  doing something that was very different  so the question you have to ask yourself  is whether it was necessarily the  variables in the study itself that  caused the beneficial adaptations or  just the fact that the program was  different from what they've been doing  before and Kylee he doesn't he doesn't  come down on the totally like nihilistic  extreme that nothing is better or worse  than anything else but he does he does  rightly point out that just just simply  novelty getting people to do stuff they  haven't been doing previously seems to  be one of the biggest factors so to wrap  up this video in general for most people  most of the time higher frequency  full-body training  tends to be better it it tends to have  some advantages that lower frequency  split type training doesn't but at the  same time just because it may be better  for most people most of the time and it  may be better for you right now that  doesn't necessarily mean that it's  better for you say three four months  from now if you plateau with that level  of training frequency then at that point  you probably would be better off dialing  it back using a split routine and if  you've been using a split routine for a  while and you've plateaued  you'd probably benefit from ramping your  training volume up so um yep short  answer it's not really the question you  should be asking in the first place so I  hope you enjoyed this video and if you  did please like and subscribe and hey  this is this is two videos in the same  week not not four months between these  last two so I'll see you in the next  video and yeah maybe maybe  it will also be within the next week  I'll just see all right bye
 

